00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.440
And we'll dive into those
few words in a moment.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.535
So to give a quick
example, this is--

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:18.460
so this is a video of
the earth's oceans.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.340
This is a video of a simulation
of the earth's oceans.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.200
This was created
on a supercomputer

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.730
in some institution in Colorado.

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:27.430
And it is actually
quite scaled down.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:29.470
You can see a lot of
interesting detail here.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.380
You can see the tendrils
coming off of Japan there.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:36.467
You can see tides everywhere
there's an enclosed area.

00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:37.300
This is scaled down.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.490
This actually is about to
two-petabyte simulation

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:40.730
in total.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.210
And this kind of data
is what oceanographers

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:46.120
help to understand--
help them understand

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.110
how the oceans
work, which in turn,

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:50.020
helps us understand
things like erosion,

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:53.917
also things like how heat moves
throughout the earth, which

00:05:53.917 --> 00:05:55.750
drastically affects
things like our climate.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:12.230
So we simulate our planet,
like how we saw in the video.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.600
We also observe it with
things like satellites

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.795
in the sky or
land-based systems.

00:06:16.795 --> 00:06:18.170
This is critical
for many fields,

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:20.700
like oceanography,
also climate science.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.240
Most things are more
immediately pragmatic,

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:24.680
things like weather prediction.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:28.070
Will there be a hurricane in
the next week on the Gulf,

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.742
or other systems like
agriculture, or insurance.

00:06:30.742 --> 00:06:32.450
Is there going to be
a drought next year?

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:33.560
How will that affect crops?

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.101
How will that affect regional
instability in northern Africa?

00:06:38.426 --> 00:06:40.300
However, once we have
those large data sets--

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.799
sorry, come back
here for a minute--

00:06:44.154 --> 00:06:46.470
once we have these
large data sets--

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:50.150
these are petabytes each
for the simulations.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:53.270
NASA has missions that
launch somewhere between one

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:55.580
and tens of petabytes
per year coming

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.880
and in as satellite imagery.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.710
Once we have those data sets,
we now need to analyze them.

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:04.130
Typically this is done by
querying some large data

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:07.160
center, pulling off a small bit
of data to your local laptop,

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.290
and then using some
of the natural Python

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.020
tools like NumPy,
pandas, and scikit-learn

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:13.987
interactively with
Jupyter Notebooks.

00:07:13.987 --> 00:07:16.070
This is the workflow that
we're really focused on.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.810
We're focused on the analytics
side of these large earth

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:20.095
science data sets.

00:07:20.095 --> 00:07:21.872
There are some challenges here.

00:07:21.872 --> 00:07:23.330
The first is that
the data sets are

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:26.540
different from what normal
big data tools are used to.

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:28.407
There are large,
gridded data sets.

00:07:28.407 --> 00:07:29.990
Imagine the temperature,
and pressure,

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.560
and wind speed
everywhere on the earth,

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.010
in many different
altitudes over time.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:37.864
That data set doesn't
fit into a database.

00:07:37.864 --> 00:07:40.030
That's differently structured
than what you normally

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:42.040
find in most BI tooling.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.460
It's very, very natural to
use tools like NumPy, pandas

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.960
or xarray, which [INAUDIBLE]
designed for these gridded data

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:47.740
structures.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.354
But those tools have
traditionally not scaled well.

00:07:50.354 --> 00:07:51.145
There's a mismatch.

00:07:51.145 --> 00:07:52.975
We either have
scalable, big data tools

00:07:52.975 --> 00:07:55.360
that are designed for
tabular data sets.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.190
Or we have more
scientific tools that

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:01.940
are designed for
small amounts of data.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.180
So one solution we use
to solve that problem

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:07.030
is what I particularly
work on is a tool

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:12.150
called Dask, which is a
library for parallel computing

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:13.500
in Python.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:15.870
And Dask can use
other Python libraries

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.180
like NumPy or like
pandas, produced scalable

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:19.560
variants of those libraries.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.570
It allows you to scale
up a Python ecosystem

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.280
to the 100-terabyte
scale fairly naturally.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:27.220
We'll see an example
of that in a moment.

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:29.946
The other challenge we
face is increased access.

00:08:29.946 --> 00:08:32.070
So some people have access
to these supercomputers,

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.080
some people have access
to these data centers,

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.159
but relatively few.

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:37.200
You can imagine there
might be a graduate student

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.200
in the Philippines with
fairly low resources

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:41.789
both in terms of
global connectivity.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:44.397
He can't download the
petabytes from Colorado.

00:08:44.397 --> 00:08:46.230
He also may not have
the financial resources

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:48.560
to buy all the credits on that.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:49.935
And so we need to
create services

00:08:49.935 --> 00:08:53.910
that allow them to run their
companies as remotely, bringing

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:56.020
the users to the data.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:58.410
This is a problem that
many organizations face.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:08:59.400
We also face it.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.360
And we're going to solve
our problem with JupyterHub.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:07.135
Some people may be
familiar with Jupyter.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:13.630
And JupyterHub allows
an administrator

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:15.942
to easily provide Jupyter
instances to many people

00:09:15.942 --> 00:09:16.900
within an organization.

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:19.370
And we're going to see an
example of that as well.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:23.650
So I'm going to show an example
here of pangeo.pydata.org.

00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:26.490
And if you want to, you
can go to this website

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:29.015
now, pangeo.pydata.org.

00:09:29.015 --> 00:09:30.640
This is a JupyterHub
deployment running

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.170
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.040
You can sign in with
GitHub credentials.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.610
And when you sign in, you have
the option to create a server.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.265
We provide you with your own
personal Jupyter Notebook

00:09:39.265 --> 00:09:40.352
server.

00:09:40.352 --> 00:09:42.010
Let's skip forward a little bit.

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:44.680
As we wait here,
Google Cloud Platform

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:48.010
is actually spinning up
for us some node somewhere

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:49.270
on the cloud.

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:52.059
That will take a little bit.

00:09:52.059 --> 00:09:54.100
And we're dropped here
into a JupyterLab session.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.350
So JupyterLab is
the next generation

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:57.611
of the Jupyter Notebook.

00:09:57.611 --> 00:09:59.110
It has the normal
Notebook interface

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:00.318
on the right that you've see.

00:10:00.318 --> 00:10:02.410
It also has things
like a file browser.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.250
You can open up a terminal.

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:06.627
You have a full Linux
workstation here.

00:10:06.627 --> 00:10:08.710
And you can do things like
manage your environment

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:12.147
with your [INAUDIBLE] libraries,
[INAUDIBLE] libraries.

00:10:12.147 --> 00:10:14.230
And this is very, very
nice on a personal machine.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:15.750
I use it daily.

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.500
But it is very,
very valuable when

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:20.330
you're on some remote system,
[INAUDIBLE] manage that system

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:22.110
and keep that persistent.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.600
So the combination of JupyterLab
and JupyterHub we found

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:27.970
has been a really good tool to
give a variety of people access

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:29.420
to a remote system.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:33.760
And again, you can do this now
if you go to pangeo.pydata.org.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:36.400
So here, let's do a
little bit of science,

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:37.560
we're in this notebook.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:39.226
And we're going to
import some libraries

00:10:39.226 --> 00:10:40.482
like NumPy and xarray.

00:10:40.482 --> 00:10:42.940
And we're going to look at some
data stored on Google Cloud

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:43.439
Storage.

00:10:43.439 --> 00:10:47.290
We're going to look at
the sea surface height

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:50.340
of a simulation over time.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.660
We can see here we've open
up the data with xarray.

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:56.203
Xarray is a mix of NumPy
and pandas together.

00:10:56.203 --> 00:10:58.060
You have a bunch of
different variables.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:01.490
One of these variable,
the sea level altitude,

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:02.380
which is sizable.

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:03.100
It's not huge.

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:08.510
And it's chunked up into various
blocks on Google Cloud Storage.

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:12.210
So the data set is too large to
work with on a single machine.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:15.320
So we're going to use Dask
and Kubernetes to give us

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.520
more computational power.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:22.249
We're going to ask for,
I think, 40 workers.

00:11:22.249 --> 00:11:24.290
And then again, we're
using Google Cloud Platform

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:26.300
to rapidly scale this
user's environment

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:31.284
to now include many, many more
machines on that same cluster.

00:11:31.284 --> 00:11:32.950
You're going to have
to connect to that.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:34.940
And then we're going
to open up some data.

00:11:37.946 --> 00:11:39.570
So we're going to
open up this data set

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:41.290
and look at just one day of it.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:44.560
So this is showing us the
sea level altitude over time,

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:49.500
over the globe at
fairly fine resolution.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:50.810
And so what you can see here--

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:52.790
it's a little washed
out here, unfortunately.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:55.080
What you can see here is--

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:58.747
let's see-- this is the earth.

00:11:58.747 --> 00:12:01.080
And you might be able to see
the continents on the left.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:01.705
There's Africa.

00:12:01.705 --> 00:12:05.040
And there's some wiggles
coming off of Africa.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:08.730
And the red and the
blue are differences

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:11.089
in altitude of the oceans.

00:12:11.089 --> 00:12:13.130
So you see areas where
the ocean might be higher,

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:13.800
it might be lower.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:15.930
If you live in a low-lying
area like the Netherlands

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:17.888
or the Philippines, you
might be very concerned

00:12:17.888 --> 00:12:20.490
about that altitude.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:22.530
That data set's
around 70 gigabytes.

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:23.810
It's not huge.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:27.795
But it's bigger than what we'd
want to work over a laptop.

00:12:27.795 --> 00:12:30.640
We're going to do a few
computations with that data.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:33.330
We're going to aggregate
over spatial dimensions

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:36.700
versus time average, aggregate
over [INAUDIBLE] longitudes.

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:38.454
We have both time and latitude.

00:12:38.454 --> 00:12:39.870
And an average of
over [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:43.150
spatial over all the
[INAUDIBLE] time.

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:44.680
Before we do the
computation, we're

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:46.690
going to open up
the Dask dashboard

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:48.520
so we get a sense
of that computation

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:50.820
and what's happening.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:54.664
So this is telling us the
activity of our cluster

00:12:54.664 --> 00:12:55.580
as we're running with.

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:58.100
So again, Dask is going
to give us the ability

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:02.490
to scale standard NumPy
and pandas-like workloads

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:04.907
onto many machines on a cluster.

00:13:04.907 --> 00:13:06.740
Here, we're defining
some of those workloads

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:10.790
with the xarray library, which
is useful for geosciences.

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:12.440
And now we're computing that.

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:16.040
So the busy plot on the
right shows the activity

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:18.290
of all of the cores
of our cluster.

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:20.180
Every horizontal
line corresponds

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:21.347
to the activity of one core.

00:13:21.347 --> 00:13:22.763
And over time, you
can see they're

00:13:22.763 --> 00:13:24.122
working on different things.

00:13:24.122 --> 00:13:25.580
So one color, like
the green, might

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:28.160
correspond to loading data
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.660
Another color, like
the light green,

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:32.670
might correspond to
computing some aggregation.

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.090
Red is communication
between nodes.

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:37.370
And so what we've done
is we've broken down

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:40.280
this large computation into
many thousands of small NumPy

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:41.990
computations.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:43.760
And then Dask and
Kubernetes are working

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:46.202
through all those
computations for us.

00:13:46.202 --> 00:13:48.342
And what that gives
us, that gives us now--

00:13:48.342 --> 00:13:50.300
we've computed these
three different data sets.

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:52.799
We're going to look at the
aggregations all throughout.

00:13:52.799 --> 00:13:54.590
First, we're going to
expand to full screen

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:56.850
so we get the full image.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:59.225
And so we've taken all
the spatial dimensions.

00:13:59.225 --> 00:14:00.847
And we're now just
looking at time.

00:14:00.847 --> 00:14:02.680
And we're going to see
two different things.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:06.540
One, we can see some
oscillation seasonally.

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:09.020
The Earth breathes a
little bit with the oceans.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:11.740
You also see a
standard upward trend.

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:13.942
So sea level is rising.

00:14:13.942 --> 00:14:15.150
And what's really nice here--

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:17.207
I'm going to pause
this just for a moment.

00:14:17.207 --> 00:14:19.290
What's really nice here
is that we didn't actually

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:22.200
need to rely on an agency
or trust any organization

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:23.430
to give us this analysis.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:26.460
All of the raw data was
available to us on the cloud.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:28.250
It's publicly available.

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:31.050
And we were able to, with
our own computations,

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:33.510
go to that data set and do
that analysis on our own.

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:36.750
It removes any sense of
politics from the conversation

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:39.370
of whether or not sea
levels might be rising.

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:42.270
So it really democratizes
a lot of the ability

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:45.760
of anyone on the globe
currently to analyze

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.430
these sorts of data sets.

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:51.179
If we take that same analysis
and throw in latitude--

00:14:56.269 --> 00:14:58.560
the slow part here is actually
rendering the image, not

00:14:58.560 --> 00:14:59.754
the computation.

00:14:59.754 --> 00:15:01.170
So if we throw in
latitude, we can

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:04.770
see how sea level rise will
change over time on the x-axis,

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:06.800
but also how it
varies over latitude.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:10.140
You can see, for example,
there's a strong difference

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:12.162
here at 37 degrees latitude.

00:15:12.162 --> 00:15:13.620
There is a strong
difference, which

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.970
is actually, coincidentally
right where we are.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:18.120
So the sea level rise
variation increases

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:19.750
quite a bit in that latitude.

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:22.610
And here's variation over time.

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:26.370
As you can see on the eastern
coast of a lot of land masses,

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.360
there is lot of
increased variability.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:30.130
You can see a lot of
increase and decrease

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:32.670
of the sea level rise over time.

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:35.960
So again, these are just some
examples of how you can, today,

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:37.910
right now, if you ignore
the rest of the talk,

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.100
you can go run this
analysis on your own

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:42.890
with open data sets
living on Google Cloud.

00:15:42.890 --> 00:15:44.780
With computation
on Google Cloud,

00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:53.560
this whole computation cost
us something like $0.50.

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:55.750
So this workflow is
scalable, but it's also

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:56.750
very, very familiar.

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:58.750
So if you're familiar
with the Python ecosystem,

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:01.450
the tools like NumPy,
pandas, xarray, and Jupyter

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:02.810
are very familiar to you.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:04.460
And because of that,
this architecture

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:06.715
has been redeployed in many
different environments.

00:16:06.715 --> 00:16:08.730
This has had great success.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:11.599
This combination of scaling with
Jupyter and scaling with Dask

00:16:11.599 --> 00:16:13.390
and the rest of the
computational ecosystem

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:15.290
has been wildly
successful, especially

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:18.020
in the scientific communities.

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.230
So this is very commonly
deployed on HPC systems,

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:22.290
for example, to
institutions like NASA,

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:24.510
or the National Center
for Atmosphere Research,

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:26.770
with the UK Met
Office where they just

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:29.810
deployed JupyterHub and Dask
on an HPC job scheduler.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.659
And they run on some
sort of data center.

00:16:32.659 --> 00:16:34.950
This is great because these
institutions already exist.

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:36.450
It's a little bit
challenging though

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:39.110
because they all have their
own different data sets.

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:41.670
And that siloing of data
sets can be quite challenging

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:44.530
when you have to move
large data sets around.

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:47.000
Fortunately, the
same exact stack

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:50.410
also works on the cloud
with tools like Kubernetes.

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:52.000
And it works on
any cloud platform

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.234
that supports Kubernetes,
like Google Cloud Platform.

00:16:54.234 --> 00:16:56.650
And what's nice here is that
because many institutions are

00:16:56.650 --> 00:16:59.230
all placing their data
sets on the cloud,

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:02.440
suddenly all these computations
become much more universally

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:02.980
available.

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:04.690
You can start mix and matching.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:08.680
You can combine your
oceanographic data sets

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:12.520
with your population
movement data sets.

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:14.560
And it's all publicly
available, so it's

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.140
very pleasant to start
combining those things.

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:20.390
So just briefly, some
successes and some

00:17:20.390 --> 00:17:22.079
challenges we've run into.

00:17:22.079 --> 00:17:24.589
So this has been really
started as a collaboration

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:27.050
between a science
group at Columbia,

00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:29.177
a data infrastructure
group at the National

00:17:29.177 --> 00:17:31.760
Center for Atmospheric Research,
and some open source software

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:33.050
developers in Anaconda.

00:17:33.050 --> 00:17:36.260
But it's rapidly spread
out to other groups, so

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:38.184
many government
organizations and also

00:17:38.184 --> 00:17:39.850
many different other
scientific domains.

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:42.560
What I showed up above
was just for geosciences,

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:44.972
but it's equally applicable
to genomics, or to imaging.

00:17:44.972 --> 00:17:46.430
Those communities
have reached out.

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:51.770
It's also applicable to many
more for-profit enterprises.

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.770
So NumPy and pandas are used in
geosciences and everywhere else

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:56.480
as well.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:59.420
Scale, so scientists are
regularly interacting

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:00.979
with 100-terabyte data sets.

00:18:00.979 --> 00:18:02.770
When I say interact,
I don't mean computing

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:04.340
on 100-terabyte data sets.

00:18:04.340 --> 00:18:06.200
I mean they've opened
up a Jupyter Notebook,

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:09.237
and they're typing at that
notebook, and they hit Enter,

00:18:09.237 --> 00:18:10.820
and they expect to
get a response back

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:11.784
within a minute or so.

00:18:11.784 --> 00:18:13.700
So they're actually doing
interactive analysis

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:18.960
with these large complex data
sets at human time scales.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:22.816
That's really important for
the innovation of science.

00:18:22.816 --> 00:18:24.190
And we're also
doing the science.

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:26.717
So the people at Columbia who
are designed to do this work

00:18:26.717 --> 00:18:27.550
are doing this work.

00:18:27.550 --> 00:18:31.710
And they're coming
up with new analyses.

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:33.710
And the cloud, in particular,
has been very nice

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:36.200
because autoscaling has been
very helpful to reduce costs

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:37.280
without maintenance.

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:40.930
We actually don't pay
anybody to manage the system

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:42.720
that you can play with now.

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:43.340
It's up there.

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:44.199
It's live.

00:18:44.199 --> 00:18:45.365
It breaks very infrequently.

00:18:45.365 --> 00:18:48.470
And it's usually my fault.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:50.724
And it's relatively
cheap, these analyses are.

00:18:50.724 --> 00:18:52.640
Because we can scale our
clusters up and down,

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:55.310
we can burst out to
a 1,000 machines,

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:57.380
and we can go back down
again to zero again.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:01.730
And we can be fairly
cheap in that way.

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:05.060
This has also been pretty
impressive for access.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:07.090
So if you watch people
who log in, a lot of them

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:08.080
are graduate students,
a lot of them

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:09.670
undergraduates,
all of them aren't

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:11.410
in resource-rich countries.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:14.380
And it's been very
exciting to see

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:16.600
this large-scale
analysis be taken out

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:19.424
into those other communities.

00:19:19.424 --> 00:19:21.750
However, there's also
a lot of challenges.

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:23.450
So we're struggling
with interactivity

00:19:23.450 --> 00:19:25.820
on petabyte-scale data sets.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.737
So Dask is underlying
parallels in machinery--

00:19:28.737 --> 00:19:30.820
seems to be struggling to
operate interactive time

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:32.990
scales in both sizes.

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:36.740
Data formats, so the established
formats for gridded data,

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:40.282
things like HDF5, don't work
very well in cloud storage.

00:19:40.282 --> 00:19:41.240
There are alternatives.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:42.830
But now we have
to build consensus

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:44.570
among a lot of
scientific communities

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:48.170
to establish new common
data formats designed

00:19:48.170 --> 00:19:51.617
for infrequent random access.

00:19:51.617 --> 00:19:53.950
There's a general lack of
expertise among the science IT

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:54.910
community.

00:19:54.910 --> 00:19:57.770
There's a ton of people who
are well-trained to handle

00:19:57.770 --> 00:19:59.170
HPC clusters.

00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:01.990
And there's relatively few
people in the science community

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:04.780
who have the
cloud-native skill set.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:08.100
Some retraining process
needs to be done.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:11.120
And then finally, there's
resource management.

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:13.140
So things like
JupyterHub or Kubernetes

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:16.405
lack some of the
fine-grained user management

00:20:16.405 --> 00:20:18.990
around software environments,
resource limits, data sharing,

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:20.390
and governance.

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:22.890
They're tricky if you want to
manage a thousand users all

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:28.770
hammering a cluster with their
own particular resource queues.

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:30.600
So taking off my
open source hat,

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:33.004
putting on my enterprise
hat, Anaconda Enterprise

00:20:33.004 --> 00:20:34.670
saw these sorts of
problems and actually

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:37.170
didn't understand what
these problems were before I

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:39.000
built a system like this.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:40.920
It was hard to understand
all the pain that

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:44.530
goes around stopping the users
from breaking the cluster

00:20:44.530 --> 00:20:46.590
before you have a bunch
of users actively trying

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:47.381
to break a cluster.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:52.399
So that's it for my bit.

00:20:52.399 --> 00:20:54.190
I'm going to hand it
off Mario in a moment.

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:55.470
But give it a try.

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.180
So you go to pangeo.pydata.org.

00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:59.880
You can play with that
exact same analysis.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:01.470
It should all just work.

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:04.127
If you want to learn more,
you go to pangeo-data.org.

00:21:04.127 --> 00:21:06.460
If you're an interested
partner, if you want to join in,

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:09.490
there's many organizations
that come and play with us.

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:10.600
And you also get involved.

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:12.210
So we're a community
organization.

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:16.256
We do all of our communication
on GitHub, pangeo-data/pangeo.

00:21:16.256 --> 00:21:17.130
So thank you so much.

00:21:35.826 --> 00:21:38.520
MARIO LASSNIG: Yeah, it works.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:39.020
OK.

00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:40.049
Hello, everyone.

00:21:40.049 --> 00:21:41.090
My name is Mario Lassnig.

00:21:41.090 --> 00:21:43.190
I'm with the
European Organization

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:46.002
for Nuclear Research,
commonly known as CERN.

00:21:46.002 --> 00:21:47.960
Thank you, Karan, for
giving me the possibility

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:49.370
to speak here to you.

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:52.550
And I do this on behalf of
the ATLAS collaboration.

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:54.890
So ATLAS is one
of the experiments

00:21:54.890 --> 00:21:55.910
that we host at CERN.

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:03.440
OK, maybe I should stand here.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:06.430
So just a little
introduction, CERN

00:22:06.430 --> 00:22:09.160
is a particle
physics research lab

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:14.020
at the border of Switzerland and
France, beautiful Lake Geneva.

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:19.240
And our accelerator complex
is 100 meters underground.

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:21.910
You have the Large
Hadron Collider, the LHC.

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:24.430
You have the 27-kilometer
circumference.

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:27.340
And you have four
experiments, four detectors

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:29.620
attached to the LHC.

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:31.900
And we basically make
particle beams collide

00:22:31.900 --> 00:22:34.120
in the center of
these detectors.

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:38.070
And our experiment
is the ATLAS one.

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:42.870
So, this is what an
example event looks like.

00:22:42.870 --> 00:22:46.040
So this is a cross-section
of the detector.

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:49.160
It's layered like an
onion, different types

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:54.590
of things that we can measure,
most importantly the energy

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:58.290
deposits over time of
the decaying particles.

00:22:58.290 --> 00:22:59.990
So what we get--

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:02.120
the yellow dots that
you see are essentially

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:03.340
hits in the detector.

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:05.900
So you can imagine
this is a camera.

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:09.990
And it takes pictures
every 25 nanoseconds.

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:13.610
So that's a lot of data
coming out of there.

00:23:13.610 --> 00:23:16.640
If I'm not mistaken, it's
roughly 1.7 petabytes

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:17.960
per second.

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:23.060
And then we trigger that down in
hardware to event sizes of two

00:23:23.060 --> 00:23:26.110
to eight megabytes in size.

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:30.100
So this is just raw data
coming from the detector.

00:23:30.100 --> 00:23:34.330
But computing at CERN has a
quite longer history than that.

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:38.170
On the left-hand side, you
can see the original machine

00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:42.520
that Tim Berners-Lee used to
create the original technology

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:43.840
for the World Wide Web.

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:46.202
It's now in our museum.

00:23:46.202 --> 00:23:47.410
And actually, it still works.

00:23:47.410 --> 00:23:51.670
They had an event two years ago
where they tried it out again.

00:23:51.670 --> 00:23:53.420
Then we improved a bit.

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:58.440
So our data center right now is,
I believe, at the 250,000 cores

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:03.970
that we provide 300 petabytes of
disk and 200 petabyte of tape.

00:24:03.970 --> 00:24:05.290
Yes, we still use tape.

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:07.971
But eventually,
all of this is just

00:24:07.971 --> 00:24:09.220
the infrastructure underneath.

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:12.250
What we actually want
is plots like that

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:13.280
on the right-hand side.

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:16.270
This is what the people
are actually interested in.

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:18.040
And if you're a
particle physicist,

00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:20.577
you can see the
Higgs boson in there.

00:24:20.577 --> 00:24:22.160
Well, you might have
to zoom in a bit.

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:29.080
So how do we do the
computing for the experiment?

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:33.190
We leverage what we call
the WLCG, the Worldwide LHC

00:24:33.190 --> 00:24:34.315
Computing Grid.

00:24:34.315 --> 00:24:39.640
Now, CERN is not the only
center providing capabilities.

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:43.850
We have 170 data centers all
over the world, 40 countries,

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:47.500
all together, 800,000 cores,
some disks, some tape.

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:49.870
And the aggregate
network that we can use

00:24:49.870 --> 00:24:52.239
is roughly three terabytes.

00:24:52.239 --> 00:24:53.530
It's always a tiered structure.

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:55.960
So we have tier
zero, which is CERN.

00:24:55.960 --> 00:25:00.820
And then the large data centers,
Brookhaven National Laboratory,

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:03.670
Rutherford Appleton
Laboratory, all those kinds

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:05.242
of very big supporters
are involved.

00:25:05.242 --> 00:25:06.950
And then we have to
tier twos and threes.

00:25:06.950 --> 00:25:08.860
Those are universities
and laboratories

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:12.907
within the National Research
Council's funding agencies

00:25:12.907 --> 00:25:14.740
that are attached to
the larger data center.

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:16.750
It's really a tiered structure.

00:25:16.750 --> 00:25:18.910
And what we do is
heterogeneous computing.

00:25:18.910 --> 00:25:23.430
So our data centers
are publicly funded.

00:25:23.430 --> 00:25:27.930
So it's not like
somebody in Spain

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:31.860
makes a decision to
buy Intel processors

00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:33.720
and then everybody in
the world has to buy

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:34.803
exactly the same hardware.

00:25:34.803 --> 00:25:40.020
No, every single data center
have their own funding

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:42.370
structure, their own
decision making process.

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:46.710
So it's really everybody can
do what they want, literally.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:50.820
And we have to make sure within
the framework of the WLCG

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:53.220
to make it work together.

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:56.100
And not only that, the
data centers not only

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:57.690
support the ATLAS
experiment, but also

00:25:57.690 --> 00:25:59.620
many different
experiments as well.

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:02.963
And then pledge to
the experiments.

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:10.740
The distributed computing
project for ATLAS

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:15.450
takes two concepts, workflow
management and data management,

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:17.469
and merges them together.

00:26:17.469 --> 00:26:19.260
We basically scheduled
computational tasks.

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:22.230
It's really like a huge batch
farm across all those data

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:23.250
centers.

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:25.140
It interacts with the compute.

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:28.330
And the data management is in
charge of all the experiment

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:28.830
data.

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:32.210
And you can see from the curve
on the left-hand side that we

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:34.320
are roughly at--

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:37.400
we're approaching 400 petabytes
of experiment data there.

00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:40.470
Now, this includes
user output data

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:42.300
to detector data, everything.

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:45.100
But we also have a very large
operations and support team.

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:47.800
And without these people,
nothing would work.

00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:51.420
So they do the databases,
the monitoring, the analytics

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:52.528
for the experiments.

00:26:57.080 --> 00:27:00.110
Now I would like to quickly
touch the data management

00:27:00.110 --> 00:27:05.950
system because that's where
I'm primarily involved in.

00:27:05.950 --> 00:27:08.390
When we think data
management, for science,

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:12.160
it's really completely
different to a business use case

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:17.020
because the science workflows,
as Matt already explained,

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:19.010
they're a bit complicated.

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:23.140
So what we try to do is we try
to have this global namespace

00:27:23.140 --> 00:27:26.020
that we can federate across all
the different storage systems

00:27:26.020 --> 00:27:28.240
that the countries provide.

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:31.300
We need to control and
account the data end users.

00:27:31.300 --> 00:27:34.270
We need to have some policies
and rules on the data.

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:37.550
We have to transfer data
between the data centers.

00:27:37.550 --> 00:27:40.570
We have to make sure that
when the jobs access the data,

00:27:40.570 --> 00:27:41.950
that it's available.

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:44.577
We can do popularity-based
things, replication, caching,

00:27:44.577 --> 00:27:45.660
all those kinds of things.

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:47.034
And then we also
have to interact

00:27:47.034 --> 00:27:50.860
with all the other systems
that actually need the data.

00:27:50.860 --> 00:27:53.980
So the system is called Rucio.

00:27:53.980 --> 00:27:57.730
And this is the one that
we're actually integrated

00:27:57.730 --> 00:27:59.560
with the Google Cloud Platform.

00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:02.750
So I think in the
interest of time,

00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:04.780
I will just skip
to the next slide

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:07.810
because that's actually
where it gets interesting.

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:11.230
When we do orchestrated
storage-to-storage transfers,

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:15.460
so basically copying a couple
of petabytes from France

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:20.770
to the United States,
we have to hop

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:23.650
through many different
research networks.

00:28:23.650 --> 00:28:27.130
And as you can see, we
do roughly five petabytes

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:32.550
of transfers per week, all of
different types of activities.

00:28:32.550 --> 00:28:36.040
But when the data is
there, the actual jobs

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:37.370
need to do something with them.

00:28:37.370 --> 00:28:39.740
So the job IO we get
per week is much,

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:42.646
much higher than the actual
orchestrated activities.

00:28:42.646 --> 00:28:44.020
So the other one
we can schedule.

00:28:44.020 --> 00:28:46.019
We have a list of data
that we want to transfer.

00:28:46.019 --> 00:28:49.420
We know roughly a month in
advance which data we actually

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:50.480
want to move.

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:54.160
But when we have users accessing
it, this is completely chaotic.

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:56.170
And as Matt showed,
you push a button,

00:28:56.170 --> 00:28:59.440
and suddenly you try to work
through a petabyte sample

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:00.580
of data.

00:29:00.580 --> 00:29:03.700
And we have to make sure that
this is properly orchestrated,

00:29:03.700 --> 00:29:06.144
not only worldwide,
but also at the lower

00:29:06.144 --> 00:29:07.435
level with the storage systems.

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:13.150
So that looks actually
quite well and it works.

00:29:13.150 --> 00:29:14.380
So what is the problem?

00:29:14.380 --> 00:29:17.020
The problem you can
see here on this plot

00:29:17.020 --> 00:29:18.220
on the left-hand side.

00:29:18.220 --> 00:29:22.540
That is the prediction
of the amount of data

00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:23.290
that we will need.

00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:27.640
And you'll see there's
a very big jump in 2025.

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:30.340
This is when we will get
the high luminosity upgrade

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:32.440
to the Large Hadron Collider.

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:35.710
So from roughly one
exabyte of data,

00:29:35.710 --> 00:29:40.150
we will jump up to four
and more exabytes of data.

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:42.920
And already now
at 400 petabytes,

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:45.550
we are having quite
a bit of trouble

00:29:45.550 --> 00:29:49.570
getting the job IO working.

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:55.160
So this data deluge, this is
not only a problem for the LHC,

00:29:55.160 --> 00:29:57.250
we see this across
many fields of science.

00:29:57.250 --> 00:29:59.290
And the main problem
there is that, of course,

00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:02.190
storage continues to be
the major cost factor.

00:30:02.190 --> 00:30:06.160
The science output rate exceeds
the infrastructure growth rate

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:09.340
at any possible rate.

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:12.010
I mean, you can't
leverage so much taxes

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:15.470
that you build many
more science centers.

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:21.600
So we call these
tactical resources.

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:24.630
It's like we want to use
these excess resources that

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:26.460
are available in the world.

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:29.730
And so ATLAS and
Google joined forces

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:34.120
to start the Data
Ocean R&amp;D project.

00:30:34.120 --> 00:30:36.600
There's this data
lake concept that's

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:39.210
very much used in
business, so we

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:41.884
tried to give it a
funny spin and just

00:30:41.884 --> 00:30:43.800
called it a data ocean
just to make it bigger.

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:51.340
There's absolutely no
meaning behind this.

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:54.900
So what we try to
do is we wanted

00:30:54.900 --> 00:30:58.590
to make it easy for the
science users in the experiment

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:02.680
to benefit from what Google
Cloud can provide us.

00:31:02.680 --> 00:31:04.920
But on the other
hand, we also wanted

00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:08.830
to learn from Google how
they do their things.

00:31:08.830 --> 00:31:10.950
So we wanted to evaluate,
adopt, and benefit

00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:15.160
from the novel technologies that
are available on the market.

00:31:15.160 --> 00:31:19.980
So at the Supercomputing
Conference last year in Denver,

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:24.460
we had our first meeting.

00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:26.730
And we started out with
a large sheet of paper

00:31:26.730 --> 00:31:29.940
and started to draw potential
scenarios what we could do.

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:35.390
And we ended up with our
first white paper on this.

00:31:35.390 --> 00:31:38.950
So the first use cases
that we wanted to do

00:31:38.950 --> 00:31:42.070
is to ensure user analysis.

00:31:42.070 --> 00:31:45.729
Imagine on the
WLCG your job runs.

00:31:45.729 --> 00:31:46.520
It produces output.

00:31:46.520 --> 00:31:49.120
It writes it somewhere
in the world.

00:31:49.120 --> 00:31:56.280
But for example, it ends up in a
data center in a remote country

00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:57.660
with not so good network.

00:31:57.660 --> 00:32:02.640
And the only administrator there
is a PhD student on 5% time.

00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:05.100
Data center goes down, you
have to wait three days

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:06.630
to get your job output data.

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:08.710
That is clearly
not what you want.

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:12.810
So with Google
Cloud Platform, we

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:16.620
would be able to get
100% output availability

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:20.460
by making an additional
copy on the cloud.

00:32:20.460 --> 00:32:22.470
Also, we should be
able to overflow

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:25.640
the CPU to cloud compute.

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:27.390
And the other thing
that we wanted to test

00:32:27.390 --> 00:32:29.490
is the data
placement replication

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:30.710
and popularity thing.

00:32:30.710 --> 00:32:35.250
We really want to dynamically
expand the storage capacity,

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:38.850
use the additional network
that Google provides us

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:42.000
between our national
research networks.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:46.200
For example, we can inject
data in our data centers

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:51.720
in Amsterdam and have it use
the Google network to move it

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:57.550
to the US without having to rely
on our own research networks.

00:32:57.550 --> 00:33:00.250
And then, of course,
within the Google network,

00:33:00.250 --> 00:33:02.660
try to replicate it even more.

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:04.930
And the third topic that
we would like to test

00:33:04.930 --> 00:33:07.150
is to get the internal
data formats that we

00:33:07.150 --> 00:33:10.270
use for the job IO itself.

00:33:10.270 --> 00:33:12.700
What we use is--

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:17.870
if you know HDF5, it's a
data format using trees.

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:19.680
We use the root system for this.

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:23.120
So what we want, we want to rip
it apart into its constituents

00:33:23.120 --> 00:33:26.900
and try to figure out what would
happen if we don't use files.

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:30.700
So eventually, we would end up
with a couple trillion events

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:31.740
that we need to process.

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:33.690
So we're clearly not there yet.

00:33:33.690 --> 00:33:35.967
So we wanted to do some
cloud-based marshalling

00:33:35.967 --> 00:33:36.800
of events from this.

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:43.620
So how do we get data
into Google Cloud Storage?

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:45.450
This is the
necessary first step.

00:33:45.450 --> 00:33:47.940
But the LHC is running,
so we can't just

00:33:47.940 --> 00:33:50.190
shut it down and do this.

00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:53.710
So we had to integrate this
transparently and on the fly

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:57.210
because downtime, so
while the LHC is running,

00:33:57.210 --> 00:33:59.260
are very, very costly.

00:33:59.260 --> 00:34:03.370
So what we try to do is to
make GCS just another data

00:34:03.370 --> 00:34:05.340
center within the WLCG.

00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:08.880
So this means within all the
existing heterogeneous data

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:10.980
centers that we
have, we had to make

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:13.560
sure it supports all
the data policies,

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:16.395
it supports the user data
access with the authorization

00:34:16.395 --> 00:34:17.670
and authentication.

00:34:17.670 --> 00:34:20.340
It must support the existing
protocols that we use

00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:23.080
and also the existing
tool chain that we have.

00:34:23.080 --> 00:34:27.730
So this was quite a bit
of development effort.

00:34:27.730 --> 00:34:32.179
So we started with
S3 free because we

00:34:32.179 --> 00:34:35.790
had full stack support
from top to the bottom.

00:34:35.790 --> 00:34:37.400
And you can see in
the center, that's

00:34:37.400 --> 00:34:39.440
the Rucio data
management system that

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:44.270
connects the WLCG sites and
the Google Cloud Storage.

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:47.179
But then we quickly figured
out that if we continue

00:34:47.179 --> 00:34:50.389
to use S3 as our primary
data access protocol,

00:34:50.389 --> 00:34:52.489
that we're running into
some serious problems

00:34:52.489 --> 00:34:58.330
because this one is rate
limited on the storage sites.

00:34:58.330 --> 00:35:01.250
And really what's
the major problem

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:03.550
is that the key
distribution is problematic.

00:35:03.550 --> 00:35:05.375
So imagine we have
our credentials

00:35:05.375 --> 00:35:08.150
to access Google Cloud Storage,
and we start to hand it out

00:35:08.150 --> 00:35:10.490
to our users, and
somebody decides

00:35:10.490 --> 00:35:12.980
to just run for a couple
of petabytes of data,

00:35:12.980 --> 00:35:16.520
we would rack up a bill
that we could never fund.

00:35:16.520 --> 00:35:20.040
So what we decided
to do is we say,

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:23.420
OK, we don't use the
compatibility layer with S3.

00:35:23.420 --> 00:35:25.655
We move to Google
Cloud Platform-native

00:35:25.655 --> 00:35:27.830
client-side signed URL.

00:35:27.830 --> 00:35:33.830
And with that, we have complete
control over the integration.

00:35:33.830 --> 00:35:37.280
So these first movements, we
did the work quite manually slow

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:38.450
and tedious to S3.

00:35:38.450 --> 00:35:41.426
But with the signed URL
support, this basically

00:35:41.426 --> 00:35:42.800
allowed us to use
to Google Cloud

00:35:42.800 --> 00:35:46.680
Platform like any other WebDAV
storage, which was really cool.

00:35:46.680 --> 00:35:50.660
And then we used this
to do user analysis

00:35:50.660 --> 00:35:56.900
input to the European West
1 and the USEAST1 storage

00:35:56.900 --> 00:35:59.000
areas in the GCP.

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:01.190
And what happened
was that we maxed out

00:36:01.190 --> 00:36:03.050
the intermediate
throughput and started

00:36:03.050 --> 00:36:06.860
to overload the
storage from the WLCG

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:11.000
because we couldn't even inject
it fast enough into Google

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:11.665
Cloud Storage.

00:36:11.665 --> 00:36:13.040
So we were quite
happy with that.

00:36:16.260 --> 00:36:20.250
When you have your data
in, you want to compute.

00:36:20.250 --> 00:36:23.250
And we have a system
built in collaboration

00:36:23.250 --> 00:36:26.430
with the University of Texas,
Arlington and Brookhaven

00:36:26.430 --> 00:36:27.550
National Laboratory.

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:28.830
It was called PanDa.

00:36:28.830 --> 00:36:31.160
And it has a component
called the Harvester.

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:34.890
So what the Harvester
does is it basically

00:36:34.890 --> 00:36:40.470
is a real-time view on the
capabilities of your cloud.

00:36:40.470 --> 00:36:44.700
And it tells you
how much usage you

00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:47.040
have from the experiment
point of view,

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:48.790
not from the infrastructure
point of view,

00:36:48.790 --> 00:36:51.340
and what is the integration
with the cloud costs

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:52.660
that we actually have.

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:57.330
So we continuously fire
up virtual machines

00:36:57.330 --> 00:37:01.290
with the actual
computational pilots in them.

00:37:01.290 --> 00:37:02.730
We can pull the VM states.

00:37:02.730 --> 00:37:04.860
We can shut them
down when necessary.

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:08.790
And we just have the Compute
Engine completely integrated

00:37:08.790 --> 00:37:11.750
into our batch workflow.

00:37:11.750 --> 00:37:14.280
And at the bottom,
the Rucio system

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:20.580
takes care of moving the data
from the WLCG storage systems

00:37:20.580 --> 00:37:23.960
into the Google Cloud Storage
through the orchestrator

00:37:23.960 --> 00:37:24.668
transfers.

00:37:29.550 --> 00:37:33.120
So we started with
ATLAS simulation jobs.

00:37:33.120 --> 00:37:34.950
When I said at the
beginning that we

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:38.060
get a lot of data
out of the detector,

00:37:38.060 --> 00:37:41.770
we even get more data
from our simulation.

00:37:41.770 --> 00:37:44.770
I think the split is
something 30% to 70%.

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:49.060
So 70% is just pure
simulated data.

00:37:49.060 --> 00:37:50.470
Now what we need
to do is we need

00:37:50.470 --> 00:37:54.550
to run these simulations on the
Google Compute Engine clusters

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:58.760
and read the IO from
the CERN storage.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:37:59.760
That was the first step.

00:37:59.760 --> 00:38:03.980
We wanted to show that we
can run the compute on Google

00:38:03.980 --> 00:38:06.200
but accessing the data at CERN.

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:11.480
And then we moved on to also
use the IO from Google Compute.

00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:13.880
In the meantime,
we decided that we

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:18.960
could try to profit from the
preemptible virtual machines.

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.530
And you can see on the
left-hand side of the plot,

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:24.850
we used the normal VMs
that are available on GCE,

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:26.600
and then we switched
to a preemptible VMs.

00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:29.600
So this basically means that
the infrastructure can take out

00:38:29.600 --> 00:38:31.920
the VM underneath you.

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:34.430
However, if you
do this, you gain

00:38:34.430 --> 00:38:35.990
a lot of dollars per event.

00:38:35.990 --> 00:38:40.100
So this was a very
positive experience there.

00:38:40.100 --> 00:38:43.460
So the ATLAS
analysis also works.

00:38:43.460 --> 00:38:47.130
We [? prove ?] the simulation
case was just a first step.

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:52.040
We can use GCE with IO from
CERN, with IO from GCS,

00:38:52.040 --> 00:38:54.240
and its completely
and fully automated.

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:57.650
So the physicists
themselves don't even

00:38:57.650 --> 00:38:59.930
know that their jobs
are running on Google

00:38:59.930 --> 00:39:02.100
or that the data is
used from Google.

00:39:05.850 --> 00:39:09.340
So what is the
take-home message?

00:39:09.340 --> 00:39:12.300
The science workflows and
the software that we use

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:18.090
are complex and complicated
to a point where we can't just

00:39:18.090 --> 00:39:21.060
go ahead and change
things because we need

00:39:21.060 --> 00:39:23.980
to have repeatable experiments.

00:39:23.980 --> 00:39:27.760
So if we decide to
make an addition,

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:31.620
like enabling support for a
commercial cloud provider,

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:36.030
we have to make sure that
this is done in a very, very

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:37.470
conservative way.

00:39:37.470 --> 00:39:42.210
So all these workflows have
to transparently integrate

00:39:42.210 --> 00:39:44.136
these providers.

00:39:44.136 --> 00:39:45.510
But then we come
to the next step

00:39:45.510 --> 00:39:46.912
because the
scientists themselves

00:39:46.912 --> 00:39:48.120
are starting to get cautious.

00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:52.650
To a scientist, the data is
the most precious resource.

00:39:52.650 --> 00:39:55.710
They literally don't care
about the infrastructure

00:39:55.710 --> 00:39:56.800
and where it runs.

00:39:56.800 --> 00:39:59.940
But if you tell them we're
taking all your data--

00:39:59.940 --> 00:40:01.980
that is basically
your livelihood--

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:05.070
and we're putting that
on a commercial company,

00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:08.620
and it's not on publicly-funded
and science infrastructure,

00:40:08.620 --> 00:40:10.930
they start to get
very, very cautious.

00:40:10.930 --> 00:40:14.820
So what we are
trying to do here is

00:40:14.820 --> 00:40:17.280
we are trying to win the
scientists-- we're basically

00:40:17.280 --> 00:40:22.440
trying to win ourselves over
by providing ourselves tools

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:26.870
to use the commercial platforms
in a way that makes sense

00:40:26.870 --> 00:40:27.810
to us.

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:31.730
So we can allow our
existing science software

00:40:31.730 --> 00:40:36.260
to run transparently against
a commercial provider while

00:40:36.260 --> 00:40:39.920
at the same time keeping
complete control of the data.

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:43.490
So for us really,
the important part

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:47.540
was to have a data management
system to be able to do that.

00:40:47.540 --> 00:40:52.550
So imagine you're a
science community,

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:56.030
you're not on a scale
of ATLAS, you're maybe

00:40:56.030 --> 00:40:59.060
10 people, five to 10
people, and you decide,

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:02.780
OK, I have access to my
university storage cluster.

00:41:02.780 --> 00:41:08.270
But I would really like to get
some capability from Google

00:41:08.270 --> 00:41:13.920
Storage completely
integrated into my workflow,

00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:17.350
you can use our Rucio
system to do this.

00:41:17.350 --> 00:41:20.040
You can use the PanDa and
Harvester system to do this.

00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:23.220
So we really give
you the possibility

00:41:23.220 --> 00:41:28.200
here to integrate your existing
on-premise capabilities

00:41:28.200 --> 00:41:29.850
with the commercial
cloud providers

00:41:29.850 --> 00:41:33.790
and give you as a
science customer

00:41:33.790 --> 00:41:38.660
full control over all
your collaboration data.

00:41:38.660 --> 00:41:41.210
So ATLAS and Google
are committed

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:42.380
to long-term collaboration.

00:41:42.380 --> 00:41:45.260
This is not a one-term thing.

00:41:45.260 --> 00:41:50.090
So we're really looking forward
to scaling out even more.

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:52.580
We have many more
experiments that are

00:41:52.580 --> 00:41:55.820
deploying in the same tools.

00:41:55.820 --> 00:42:00.890
And we are giving them
essentially the capability

00:42:00.890 --> 00:42:04.970
for free to hook into the
commercial cloud providers

00:42:04.970 --> 00:42:09.530
and use it as part of
their science workflows.

00:42:09.530 --> 00:42:11.310
And with that, I'm at the end.

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:13.010
And thank you.

00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:14.610
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:15.110 --> 00:42:18.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

